{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-120072",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/523f18dc7b081ecf6dcfa1f09c6fe715012fffba3e4e07d1dfd44c3d53cf702c.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $x=-1$ and $x=0.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}={x}^{2}(x+1),\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}={x}^{2}+x=x(x+1).\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-1$ and $x=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<-1$ | $-1<x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $\u2198$ | $\u2197$ | $\u2197$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}={x}^{2}(x+1).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{\u2032}={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
